From: At30c <At30c@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] sched/ems: port the ecs dynamic governor from s5e9965

Replace the fixed saving mode of the core sparing governor with the ecs
dynamic governor of the S5E9965 EMS (ecs_dynamic.c of the S947B kernel
source, rewritten on the Exynos 990 EMS). Every tick each ECS domain
gets the number of cpus it needs: its busy cpus, plus the tasks that
overflow the smaller domains that help it (nr-help, +1 when all of them
are busy), plus their misfit tasks above help-misfit-thr (misfit-help).
A domain with a spared cpu is never busy and gives no nr help while it
can still grow, so the smaller clusters fill first. Cpus are taken one
at a time, so a single Mongoose core can serve one heavy thread, which
the old mode could not give before all six little/mid cpus were busy.

The S5E9965 reads runnable and misfit averages from its multi load
tracker (time weighted over each 4 ms tick); here they are moving
averages sampled once per tick from rq->nr_running and the load
balancer misfit status, so nothing is added to the enqueue/dequeue
path. Cpu capacity follows the policy max, as the energy table is
rebuilt on thermal/SIOP limits.

The emstune ecs node takes the S5E9965 per-set parameters
(dynamic-busy-ratio, dynamic-nr-run-up-ratio, dynamic-help-misfit-thr,
min-cpus); -1 or absent keeps the /ems/ecs/dynamic defaults. Med boost
sets keep the mid cores. Unlike the S5E9965 layout, little misfit tasks
wake a mid core and only mid misfit tasks wake a Mongoose one.
/sys/kernel/ems/ecs/dynamic shows the state; ecs_dynamic_cpu_info and
ecs_dynamic_domain_info trace it with the S5E9965 format.
---
